<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">[PM request acknowledged, consider this a placeholder]- will critique shortly :)</span></p>
<p>Hello Identity8866! let's take a look :)</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Page Layout (Optional): It'd begin as a tale. After a medium amount of writing in the tale, it'd then show an entry of the SCP - at least, what is understood about them at that point. Then switching back to tale, then after more is learnt, the document is updated again. Repeat this until the document is fully written, ending with a short note.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>While I'm not the best person to ask (or use as reference) by any means on formats. I don't really see the reason this needs to cater to a tale and an SCP article? -While the narrative does show a character writing/updating the article in the field, they don't seem to have clear motivation to do so? I feel for this to work better they need more than just &quot;its their job&quot;, as motivation.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Elevator Pitch: An unexplained (yet theorized) scientific phenomena referred to as &quot;Earthquake lights&quot;. Strange lights that seem to appear in the skies whilst (usually high-magnitude) earthquakes form. However, what isn't expected is that these are entities made of light, appearing to simply spectate the earthquakes. Why they do this is found out in the tale.</p>
<p>Central Narrative: During a high-magnitude earthquake near Site [REDACTED], there were &quot;Earthquake Lights&quot; spotted by many people. As this is a scientifically unexplained phenomena, MTF Unit Rho-10 &quot;Shaken, Not Stirred&quot; (an MTF unit created to withstand seismic activity, or unnatural geological movement) enters the scene to investigate. After recordings are taken, and the film is placed through numerous filters &amp; analysations, it's found that each of these 'strobes' of light contain a nearly humanoid entity which seems to be watching the earthquakes. When another high-magnitude earthquake occurs near the same area within the next month, the same dispatch is sent to investigate these flashes of light closer. As the protagonist, codename &quot;Charlie-0&quot; (or C-0 by military alphabets) approaches towards the light, it seems to remain still for much longer than previous. They go to the top of the nearest destroyed building via an extremely long grappling hook and a lot of climbing. 23 minutes later, they arrive at the top, before the entity slowly floats down towards him. They take him somewhere - yet to be discovered. The man would slowly begin to communicate with these entities, finding out more and more about them - as the secrets reveal he continually updates the logs manually. Finally, when he's freed, he decides to watch one final earthquake with them. That's how it'd end, with a full log &amp; a destroyed city.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So overall, theres a bit of missing clarification here. The entities 'secrets' and motives don't really get revealed or mentioned much, which makes it hard for me to gauge wether or not the payoff works with the rest of the narrative and concept. As it stands, the narrative feels a bit anticlimactic as there isn't any closure to what the skips are doing or why.</p>
<p>Along with this, the character himself isn't really developed (at least not from what I can see), which makes it hard to get invested emotionally in the narrative as whole, as he is more or less a random MTF to the audience.- what are his motives? why does he want to update the logs? does he go through any major conflicts? what will make me as the reader care about this character?</p>
<p>Its also oddly convenient to me that this activity happened in the same place, in a very short time frame, and the Foundation just happened to notice there was anomaly.- Generally speaking, the earthquakes don't seem to bare much of an impact on much? they just sort happen, and theres not real consequences.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Hook/Attention-Grabber: The fact that this could, hypothetically, be real is a big thing. Playing off of real world unexplained phenomena. Not to mention the fact it opens up as a tale-SCP has always caught my interest. A dangerous scenario (that being an earthquake) being traversed whilst also trying to gain intel on things which you can barely even see for more then a flash of a second is extremely difficult, and thrilling to read about.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Dangerous scenarios won't really keep audience attention, if theres nothing to back it conceptually (or narratively for that matter). While I think this premise could defiantly work, there doesn't seem to be enough focus on the actual anomaly and their overall motives here.</p>
<p><strong>Red or green?</strong><br />
Due to my afore mentioned concerns, no-greenlight</p>
<p>If you would like me review this (or any concept) further, then please feel free to send me a PM or ask via IRC. Response times generally range from 2-12 hours over PMs. If your looking for additional critiques/perspectives in general, then I would highly recommend contacting the butterfly squad :)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Cheers,</p>
<p>RED</p>

