<p>Hello! Thank you for answering!</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I worded it poorly. I edited the document and made it so that it specifies that it's a regular SCP, however with more of a narrative structure (think SCP-5000).<br />
I changed it so that the MTF Unit shows development by originally only interacting with the entities for research purposes, however slowly grows to empathize with the fact that they regret their nature. They seem to take a strong interest in the mental functions of these SCPs, leading to an SCP Article with purposefully too much detail - the final draft edited by scientists cuts out much more detail than what is present during it.<br />
I've added a new paragraph detailing the reasoning and secrets behind the entities, which are as follows.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>These entities don't wish to create and spectate the chaos which happens, however as if an addiction they depend on it. It is unknown whether they die if there is no chaos spectated. These light creatures are entities born from dying stars, and as such are naturally attracted towards extremely chaotic events, living off of the chaos. Due to the fact that it's one of the few places which are inhabited, these entities seem to enjoy the chaos which comes with this. When times are extremely boring, they seem to create their own manual disasters, usually by moving tectonic plates and creating earthquakes. as they're the easiest to create.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>To make the MTF Unit have more development &amp; reasons to care, I gave them a slight amount of backstory explained in this quote.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He empathizes, as he was originally a drug addict who severely struggled to break his addiction. He gains motivation to learn more about them to try and find a way to have the foundation assist in this 'chaos'-addiction, as that's what he always wished he had. This also motivates him to write more so the foundation can understand as much as they can.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It took me a moment to think of a good hook, however I decided on one. It'd be the fact that these creatures seem to have a society, and people they care about, along with emotions and feeling. It gives a side to them other than just 'chaos-lovers'. Diving deeper into the sociability &amp; lives of these creatures which have been created on a foundation of misery &amp; chaos could prove extremely interesting.</p>
</div>
